25. React Router

React Router is a library used to create navigation between different pages or views in a React application.

Normally, a React application can have many components, but everything may appear on the same URL. React Router allows us to create different URLs for different parts of the application.

For example:

/home
/products
/about
/contact

Each URL can display a different React component.

First, React Router needs to be installed:

npm install react-router-dom

A simple setup looks like:

import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from "react-router-dom";

function App() {
  return (
    <BrowserRouter>
      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
        <Route path="/products" element={<Products />} />
        <Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
      </Routes>
    </BrowserRouter>
  );
}

Here:

BrowserRouter enables React Router.
Routes contains the routes.
Route defines a URL and the component that should appear there.
path is the URL.
element is the component displayed for that URL.

For example:

/          → Home
/products  → Products
/about     → About
26. Multiple Pages

A React application can have different components that act like different pages.

For example, we can create:

src/
├── App.jsx
├── Home.jsx
├── Products.jsx
├── About.jsx
└── Contact.jsx

Home.jsx:

function Home() {
  return <h1>Home Page</h1>;
}

export default Home;

Products.jsx:

function Products() {
  return <h1>Products Page</h1>;
}

export default Products;

Then they can be connected using React Router:

import Home from "./Home";
import Products from "./Products";

function App() {
  return (
    <BrowserRouter>
      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
        <Route path="/products" element={<Products />} />
      </Routes>
    </BrowserRouter>
  );
}

Now the application has two different pages:

http://localhost:5173/
http://localhost:5173/products

The important idea is that these are still part of the same React application. React Router changes what component is displayed depending on the URL.

27. Route Parameters

Sometimes we don't want every product to have its own manually written route.

For example, we might have:

/products/1
/products/2
/products/3

Instead of creating a separate route for every product, we can use a route parameter.

<Route path="/products/:id" element={<ProductDetail />} />

:id is a parameter.

For example:

/products/1

means:

id = 1

And:

/products/25

means:

id = 25

Inside the component, we can get the parameter using useParams():

import { useParams } from "react-router-dom";

function ProductDetail() {
  const { id } = useParams();

  return <h1>Product ID: {id}</h1>;
}

If the user visits:

/products/5

the page can display:

Product ID: 5

This becomes especially useful when working with APIs.

For example, we could use the ID to request:

GET /products/5

and display the details of product 5.

28. Navigation

We need a way for users to move between pages.

React Router provides the Link component for this.

import { Link } from "react-router-dom";

function Navbar() {
  return (
    <nav>
      <Link to="/">Home</Link>
      <Link to="/products">Products</Link>
      <Link to="/about">About</Link>
    </nav>
  );
}

The user can click the links to move between pages.

We generally use:

<Link to="/products">
  Products
</Link>

instead of using a normal HTML link:

<a href="/products">Products</a>

because Link is designed to work with React Router.

We can also navigate using JavaScript with useNavigate().

import { useNavigate } from "react-router-dom";

function Product() {
  const navigate = useNavigate();

  function goHome() {
    navigate("/");
  }

  return <button onClick={goHome}>Go Home</button>;
}

This can be useful when navigation should happen after an action.

For example:

User submits form
       ↓
Product is created
       ↓
Navigate to Products page
29. Layouts

A layout is a part of the application that stays the same while the page content changes.

For example, a website might always have:

--------------------------------
        My Website
--------------------------------
Home | Products | About
--------------------------------

        Page Content

--------------------------------
          Footer
--------------------------------

The navigation bar and footer do not need to be recreated for every page.

React Router supports layouts using nested routes and Outlet.

For example:

import { Outlet } from "react-router-dom";

function Layout() {
  return (
    <>
      <nav>
        <Link to="/">Home</Link>
        <Link to="/products">Products</Link>
        <Link to="/about">About</Link>
      </nav>

      <main>
        <Outlet />
      </main>
    </>
  );
}

Then the routes can be structured like:

<Routes>
  <Route path="/" element={<Layout />}>
    <Route index element={<Home />} />
    <Route path="products" element={<Products />} />
    <Route path="about" element={<About />} />
  </Route>
</Routes>

Outlet is the place where the current page will appear.

For example:

Layout
│
├── Navbar
│
├── Outlet
│     ├── Home
│     ├── Products
│     └── About
│
└── Footer

This is useful for larger React projects because common UI can be shared between pages.

30. Multi-Page Project

After learning React Router, the student can combine everything into one small project.

A good beginner project could be a Game Library.

The application could have:

Home
Games
About

The routes could be:

/
 /games
 /games/:id
 /about

The home page could display:

Welcome to My Game Library

[View Games]

The Games page could display:

Games

Minecraft       [View]
Terraria        [View]
Stardew Valley  [View]

When the user clicks View:

/games/1

The game detail page could display:

Minecraft

Genre: Sandbox
Developer: Mojang

[Back to Games]

The project could use an API as well if the student has already learned fetch.

The overall structure could be:

App
│
└── BrowserRouter
      │
      └── Layout
            │
            ├── Navbar
            │
            ├── Home
            │
            ├── Games
            │
            ├── GameDetail
            │     └── useParams()
            │
            └── About

The student would practice:

Creating multiple React components
Creating routes
Moving between pages
Using Link
Using route parameters
Using useParams()
Creating a shared layout
Using Outlet
Connecting pages to API data
Navigating after an action with useNavigate()
Simple Exercises

Exercise 1 — Three Pages

Create:

Home
About
Contact

and give each page its own route.

Exercise 2 — Navigation Bar

Create a navigation bar with links:

Home | About | Contact

Clicking each link should display the correct page.

Exercise 3 — Product Detail

Create a route:

/products/:id

Use useParams() to display the product ID.

For example:

/products/10

should display:

Product ID: 10

Exercise 4 — Product List

Create a list of products:

const products = [
  { id: 1, name: "Keyboard" },
  { id: 2, name: "Mouse" },
  { id: 3, name: "Monitor" }
];

Display each product with a link to:

/products/1
/products/2
/products/3

Exercise 5 — Layout

Create a layout containing:

Navbar
Outlet
Footer

Make sure the Navbar and Footer remain visible when changing pages.

Exercise 6 — Navigate After Action

Create a button that performs an action and then uses useNavigate() to move to another page.

For example:

[Finish]

clicking the button should navigate to:

/success

Exercise 7 — Multi-Page Project

Create a small Game Library or Movie Library with:

/
 /games
 /games/:id
 /about

